.TH CWE-1321 7 "2026-11-15" "CWE Top 25 (2025)" "Software Weakness Reference"
.SH NAME
CWE\-1321 \- Improperly Controlled Modification of Object Prototype Attributes ('Prototype Pollution')
.SH RANK
KEV\-referenced weakness. Not part of the 2025 CWE Top 25; documented here because it appears in the CISA KEV catalog.
.SH CLASSIFICATION
Class: KEV\-referenced weakness.
.SH DESCRIPTION
The product receives input from an upstream component that specifies attributes that are to be initialized or updated in an object, but it does not properly control modifications of attributes of the object prototype. By adding or modifying attributes of an object prototype, it is possible to create attributes that exist on every object, or replace critical attributes with malicious ones. This can be problematic if the product depends on existence or non\-existence of certain attributes, or uses pre\-defined attributes of object prototype (such as hasOwnProperty, toString or valueOf). This weakness is usually exploited by using a special attribute of objects called proto, constructor or prototype. Such attributes give access to the object prototype. This weakness is often found in code that assigns object attributes based on user input, or merges or clones objects recursively.
.SH KNOWN EXPLOITED VULNERABILITIES
CISA KEV catalog (as of 2026-11-15): \fB1\fR exploited CVE mapped to this weakness. Each entry has the catalog write\-up of how it was used in the wild (not a runnable exploit) and the required remediation. Code\-level vulnerable\->fixed pairs, when curated, are under \fBEXAMPLES\fR.
.SS CVE\-2026\-34621
Adobe Acrobat and Reader, added 2026\-04\-13.
.PP
Adobe Acrobat and Reader Prototype Pollution Vulnerability.
.PP
\fIHow it is exploited (CISA):\fR
Adobe Acrobat and Reader contain a prototype pollution vulnerability that allows for arbitrary code execution.
.PP
\fIRemediation:\fR
Apply mitigations per vendor instructions, or discontinue use.
 Due 2026\-04\-27.
.PP
\fIReferences:\fR
.br
https://helpx.adobe.com/security/products/acrobat/apsb26\-43.html
.br
